 ## Proof
 **Proof technique:** direct.
 
 **Given:** $H=H^*$, $S=LL^*$, $L$ invertible, $c\ne0$, and $y=L^*c$.
 
-1.1 The matrix $\widetilde H=L^{-1}HL^{-*}$ is Hermitian, since $\widetilde H^*=(L^{-*})^*H^*(L^{-1})^* =L^{-1}HL^{-*}=\widetilde H.$ The coordinate map $c\mapsto y=L^*c$ is invertible by [F1]. [F1, F2, given, algebra]
+1.1 Taking adjoints of $L^{-1}L=I$ and $LL^{-1}=I$ by [F3] gives $L^*(L^{-1})^*=I$ and $(L^{-1})^*L^*=I$. Hence $L^*$ is invertible with inverse $(L^{-1})^*=L^{-*}$; taking adjoints again gives $(L^{-*})^*=L^{-1}$. The matrix $\widetilde H=L^{-1}HL^{-*}$ is Hermitian, since $\widetilde H^*=(L^{-*})^*H^*(L^{-1})^* =L^{-1}HL^{-*}=\widetilde H.$ The coordinate map $c\mapsto y=L^*c$ is invertible by [F1]. [F1, F2, F3, given, algebra]
 
-2.1 Substituting $c=L^{-*}y$ into $Hc=\lambda Sc$ and multiplying by $L^{-1}$ gives $L^{-1}HL^{-*}y=\lambda L^{-1}LL^*L^{-*}y=\lambda y.$ Every operation is reversible because $L$ is invertible, so the generalized and whitened eigenproblems have exactly the same eigenvalues, with eigenvectors related by $y=L^*c$. [F1, F2, step 1.1, given, algebra]
+2.1 Substituting $c=L^{-*}y$ into $Hc=\lambda Sc$ and multiplying by $L^{-1}$ gives $L^{-1}HL^{-*}y=\lambda L^{-1}LL^*L^{-*}y=\lambda y.$ Every operation is reversible because $L$ is invertible, so the generalized and whitened eigenproblems have exactly the same eigenvalues, with eigenvectors related by $y=L^*c$. [F1, F2, F3, step 1.1, given, algebra]
 
-3.1 With $c=L^{-*}y$, matrix multiplication gives $c^*Hc=y^*L^{-1}HL^{-*}y=y^*\widetilde H y,\qquad c^*Sc=y^*L^{-1}LL^*L^{-*}y=y^*y.$ Since $c\ne0$ iff $y\ne0$, division proves equality of the two Rayleigh quotients. [F1, F2, step 1.1, algebra] ∎
+3.1 With $c=L^{-*}y$, matrix multiplication gives $c^*Hc=y^*L^{-1}HL^{-*}y=y^*\widetilde H y,\qquad c^*Sc=y^*L^{-1}LL^*L^{-*}y=y^*y.$ Since $c\ne0$ iff $y\ne0$, and $S>0$ makes $c^*Sc>0$, both equal denominators are positive. Division proves equality of the two Rayleigh quotients. [F1, F2, F3, step 1.1, algebra] ∎
 
